19:T687,## The plug-adapter analogy

Your laptop has a flat plug; the wall socket in another country is round. You don't rewire the laptop and you don't rewire the wall, you slip a **travel adapter** between them. It exposes the shape the wall expects on one side and the shape your laptop has on the other, translating in the middle.

Software has the exact same problem. You want to use a third-party or legacy class, say a `LegacyAudio` whose method is `startAudio(file)`but the rest of your code is written to call `play(file)`. The shapes don't match, and you can't (or shouldn't) edit the legacy class.

## The Adapter

An **Adapter** is a wrapper that **implements the interface your code expects** (the *Target*) and **forwards each call** to the wrapped object (the *Adaptee*), translating names and arguments along the way:

```
class AudioAdapter implements MediaPlayer {
    LegacyAudio legacy;
    void play(String f){ legacy.startAudio(f); }   // translate
}
```

Your code calls `play()`; the adapter quietly turns that into `startAudio()`. **Neither the client nor the legacy class changes**, only the thin adapter in between knows about both.

## Object adapter vs class adapter

- **Object adapter** (preferred), the adapter *holds a reference* to the adaptee (composition). Flexible, works in any language, can wrap subclasses.
- **Class adapter**, the adapter *inherits* from both the target and the adaptee (multiple inheritance). Only possible where the language allows it, and more rigid.

> The Adapter keeps the rest of your code clean: callers speak one consistent interface, and all the messy translation to mismatched APIs lives in one isolated place.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"adapter"}],["$","$L18","adapter",{"lesson":{"id":"adapter","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Adapter","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Convert the interface a class HAS into the interface a client EXPECTS, a wrapper that translates calls so incompatible code can work together.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Stripe"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Interfaces don't match","code":["interface MediaPlayer { void play(String f); }","class LegacyAudio { void startAudio(String f){ ... } }","// app.play(...) ✗: LegacyAudio has no play()"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Your App is written to call play(media) on a MediaPlayer. That's the interface the rest of your code speaks.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"client","name":"App","methods":["+ play(media)"],"x":0,"y":0.4},{"id":"legacy","name":"LegacyAudio","methods":["+ startAudio(f)"],"x":1.6,"y":0.4,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["client","legacy"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["client"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"But the LegacyAudio class you want to reuse only has startAudio(f). The App can't call it directly, the method names and shape simply don't match.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["client","legacy"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["client","legacy"]},"vars":[["App expects","play()"],["legacy has","startAudio()"]]}]},{"label":"An adapter translates","code":["interface MediaPlayer { void play(String f); }","class LegacyAudio { void startAudio(String f){ ... } }","class AudioAdapter implements MediaPlayer {","    LegacyAudio legacy;","    void play(String f){ legacy.startAudio(f); }   // translate","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"MediaPlayer is the Target interface, the one the App already speaks, with play(f).","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"target","name":"MediaPlayer","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ play(f)"],"x":0.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"adapter","name":"AudioAdapter","methods":["+ play(f)"],"x":0.3,"y":1.6,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"adaptee","name":"LegacyAudio","methods":["+ startAudio(f)"],"x":2.1,"y":0.9,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"g1","from":"adapter","to":"target","kind":"implement"},{"id":"g2","from":"adapter","to":"adaptee","kind":"aggregate","label":"wraps"}],"shownClassIds":["target"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["target"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"AudioAdapter IMPLEMENTS MediaPlayer (dashed triangle), so the App can use it like any other player, it has the play() the App expects.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["target","adapter"],"shownRelIds":["g1"],"activeIds":["adapter"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"But inside, the adapter HOLDS a LegacyAudio (diamond, “wraps”) and TRANSLATES: its play() calls legacy.startAudio(). It bridges the two shapes.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["target","adapter","adaptee"],"shownRelIds":["g1","g2"],"activeIds":["adapter","adaptee"]},"vars":[["adapter","is-a Target, wraps Adaptee"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"The App speaks play(): it calls play(\"song\") on the adapter, exactly the interface it expects.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"a","label":"App","kind":"actor"},{"id":"ad","label":"AudioAdapter","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"lg","label":"LegacyAudio"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"a","to":"ad","label":"play(\"song\")","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"ad","to":"lg","label":"startAudio(\"song\")","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"lg","to":"ad","label":"ok","kind":"return"},{"id":"m4","from":"ad","to":"a","label":"done","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["App calls","play()"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"The adapter quietly translates that into the legacy call, play() forwards to startAudio(\"song\") on the wrapped LegacyAudio.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["adapter does","startAudio()"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"LegacyAudio does its work and returns ok; the adapter returns done to the App. Both sides worked together, yet neither one had to change.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["result","incompatible code, bridged ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
